h2. Summary
Remove the FC -> FC_Cloud -> use-case release cascade (mono-repo multi-module + externalized config)

h2. Context
* *FC*: config-oriented Java application, also used on-premise (no Spring, no actuator).
* *FC_Cloud*: separate Spring + Docker project, depends on FC in its pom.
* *Use-case images*: one Docker image per use case, based on FC_Cloud, containing only the configuration.

h2. Problem
Any change in FC triggers a chain of releases:
# Release FC
# Bump the FC version in the FC_Cloud pom, release FC_Cloud
# Rebuild and release every use-case image

Consequences:
* 1 code change = 2 + N releases (N = number of use cases)
* Slow delivery of fixes, including urgent ones
* Risk of version drift (use cases running different FC versions)
* Cross-project debugging and refactoring are difficult
* Manual version bumps are error-prone

h2. Proposed solution
h3. 1. One repository, multi-module Maven project
{code}
fc-parent (pom)
 |- fc-core    -> current FC, no Spring dependency (on-premise deliverable)
 |- fc-cloud   -> Spring Boot + actuator + Dockerfile, depends on fc-core
{code}
* Single version for both modules, single pipeline, single release.
* On-premise keeps using the {{fc-core}} jar only: no actuator, no cloud dependencies.

h3. 2. One FC_Cloud image, configuration injected at runtime
* Use-case configuration moves out of the images (ConfigMap / mounted volume / Spring profiles).
* Use-case images are kept only where they add code or libraries.

h2. Expected gains
||Topic||Today||After||
|Releases per FC change|2 + N|1|
|Docker images to build|N + 1|1|
|Version bumps in poms|Manual|None (same version)|
|Version drift between use cases|Possible|Not possible|
|Config change for a use case|Image rebuild|Config update only, no build|
|On-premise delivery|FC jar|fc-core jar (unchanged)|

h2. Acceptance criteria
* FC and FC_Cloud are built and released from one repository with one version.
* {{fc-core}} has no Spring/cloud dependency (checked in the build, e.g. maven-enforcer).
* On-premise deployment works with {{fc-core}} only.
* At least one use case runs on the generic FC_Cloud image with external configuration.
* A change in fc-core reaches a cloud use case with a single release.

h2. Suggested sub-tasks
# Create parent pom and migrate FC as {{fc-core}} (keep git history)
# Migrate FC_Cloud as {{fc-cloud}}
# Merge CI pipelines into one build/release
# Externalize configuration for one pilot use case
# Migrate remaining use cases
# Archive old repositories and update documentation

h2. Risks
* Git history migration of two repositories
* On-premise customers needing a different release rhythm than cloud (mitigation: maintenance branches)
* Configs containing secrets must go to a secret store, not a ConfigMap